[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-27", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 27 — where the 530 bytes per quad go: the index, not the values", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-27.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 27 - where the 530 bytes per quad go\n\n> Seed: iteration 26's S17 - the full-SF1 SUT did not fit 16 GiB at\n> ~530 B per quad; measure the footprint by attribute group before\n> touching anything. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2 (no row this\n> iteration; a measurement that names M3's lever).\n\n## Generate\n\n  On simeon (the resident host), the 35k subset's quads transacted one\n  attribute group at a time into the same db, gc x3 between groups, the\n  live heap difference per group. This workstation was checked first as\n  a 32 GB host for an SF1-proper row (S18): 19 GB wired, 43 GB in the\n  compressor, swap 91% -- not this tick.\n\n  Prediction: message content is the heavy group (3.06M strings on full\n  SF1); the reified edges are the cheap ones (short ids).\n\n## Rank\n\n    group             quads     MB   B/quad   note\n    names            19,784      9    483\n    profile          49,460     22    473\n    knows-plain     361,246     79    230     subjects shared: one eavt entry per person\n    knows-reified 1,083,738    507    491     kn:<a>:<b> entities, 3 datoms each\n    message-date     86,932     53    638\n    message-content  86,932     40    478     2.35M chars: ~70 B of the 478 is the string\n    has-creator      86,932     38    453\n    reply-of         51,932     23    472\n    likes-reified   116,463     62    556\n\n  Sum 1.86M quads, 833 MB: ~450 B per quad on this shape. Full SF1 by\n  group counts: 20.6M quads -> ~10.3 GB, against 10.9 GB used heap\n  observed in iteration 26. The 337 B of iteration 24 was a different\n  subtraction (CSV maps in the baseline, G1) and is superseded by this\n  table.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction refuted on both counts. Content is ~15% of its own group's\n    bytes; the reified edges cost the MOST per quad. What every group\n    pays is the index: each quad becomes an entry in eavt, aevt and avet\n    (persistent hash maps of maps of sets) -- three node paths, ~150 B\n    each. Values are cheap; structure is not.\n  - Reified edge properties are 40% of full SF1's heap: likes (2.19M\n    edges x 3 datoms) and knows dates (361k x 3). The LDBC mix reads the\n    like date in IC07 and the friendship date in IS3 -- two of 29\n    operations, for 4 GB.\n  - The lever is representation, not data: a value index that is an\n    array or a compact map per attribute rather than three persistent\n    nested maps per quad. That is the persisted/columnar plane the\n    physical-layer ADR already reserves (ADR-2608160100: columnar stays\n    a large object) -- M3 proper, and an engine decision.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict: MEASURED, no row. M3's lever has a name and a number: the\ncovering indexes cost ~450 B per quad; a 3x smaller representation puts\nSF1 proper on a 16 GiB host and SF3 within a 32 GB one; SF30 stays a\ndifferent plane.\n\nSeeds for iteration 28, in order:\n\n  - S19 (representation experiment): one attribute group (likes) held in\n    a compact per-attribute structure (sorted arrays of interned ids,\n    or a single map keyed by [person message]) behind the same\n    by-predicate-value / entity-attrs seam, measured for bytes per quad\n    and for IC07's latency. A prototype in the harness, not the engine.\n  - S18 (host): a 32 GB fleet host, or this workstation when it stops\n    paging, for the SF1-proper row with the current representation.\n  - S15 (coverage) and M2 (updates) as before.\n"}]
